# Components

## Composition and flexibility

- Keep components easy to use and flexible. Do not hard-code copy, links, or other consumer content; pass it through props or `children`.
- Reuse existing library components instead of raw HTML when a wrapper already exists. For example use `<P>` instead of `<p>`, not a new paragraph primitive.
- If an existing component is almost right but not composable enough, upgrade it with a clean, backwards-compatible change rather than forking or duplicating it.

## Testing

Colocate Vitest + Testing Library tests as `ComponentName.test.tsx` (or `*.test.ts` for utilities). Cover props, events, interactions, edge cases, and accessibility behaviour. Prefer accessible queries such as `getByRole`.

## Exports

Export new or updated components and their public types from `lib/main.ts`.

```ts
export { default as Heading } from '@/components/Heading/Heading'
export type { HeadingProps } from '@/components/Heading/Heading'
```

## Accessibility and GOV.UK

Follow [GOV.UK Design System](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/) for content, behaviour, and interaction. Meet a11y standards: ARIA where appropriate, keyboard access, and screen-reader compatibility. Use official GOV.UK classes and patterns; do not invent parallel styling.
